# What promoting an `IF`'s condition has to keep.
#
# The condition used to be one `Op::EvalExpr` doing the whole job: evaluate the
# expression, trace the value, validate it as exactly "0"/"1", answer the jump.
# It is now the expression's own native ops followed by `Op::Condition`, which
# is the rest -- except where `native_shape` declines the expression, and then
# it is still the whole job in one op.
#
# The rows here are transcripts rather than a coverage set: each is one program
# whose stdout, stderr and exit status are recorded byte for byte, so a line
# that appears, disappears or moves shows as a diff. The shapes they hold are a
# condition that is a comma list, one that is an operator above a call, and a
# declining condition under trace.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE, on both
# engines, before this file was written. Regenerate a row by running its
# program under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 -- ir_dual.rs refuses to run
# at all under REWRITE for that reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )
#
# Expected lines are tagged so that no line of the block can be empty, and
# trailing blanks are part of what is compared: an IF header's `*-*` echo ends
# in one, because the instruction ends at the THEN keyword.
#
# `/nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex` is `ir_dual.rs`'s own INLINE_PATH, which a
# raised condition's middle line prints. Running the same program under the
# oracle prints the oracle's own path there instead.

# 34.6, a comma list: the raiser is `eval_logical_list`'s own, per element,
# rather than the IF keyword's 34.1. `native_shape` has no `ExprKind::Logical`
# arm, so the whole condition stays one `Op::EvalExpr` and no `Op::Condition`
# is emitted behind it.
program
if 1, 'x' then nop
----
rc> 222
err>      1 *-* if 1, 'x' 
err> Error 34 running /nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex line 1:  Logical value not 0 or 1.
err> Error 34.6:  Value of logical list expression element must be exactly "0" or "1"; found "x".

# trace i over an IF whose condition is an operator above a call, for the
# intermediate lines and their order: the argument, the function's own `>F>`,
# the right operand, the operator, and the condition's `>>>` last.
# The call takes an `Op::CallExpr` addressed at `root.L`, which is the descent
# `Interp::chunk_node_at` resolves for an IF's slot.
program
trace i
zs = 'abcd'
if length(zs) > 3 then say 'long'
----
rc> 0
out> long
err>      2 *-* zs = 'abcd'
err>        >L>   "abcd"
err>        >>>   "abcd"
err>        >=>   ZS <= "abcd"
err>      3 *-* if length(zs) > 3 
err>        >V>   ZS => "abcd"
err>        >A>   "abcd"
err>        >F>   LENGTH => "4"
err>        >L>   "3"
err>        >O>   ">" => "1"
err>        >>>   "1"
err>      3 *-*   then
err>      3 *-*     say 'long'
err>        >L>       "long"
err>        >>>       "long"

# a declining condition under trace: with no `Op::Condition` behind it, the one
# `Op::EvalExpr` still owes the `>>>` and the refusal, and both are here. The
# `>>>` appears once, and the refusal is 34.1 because a single expression is
# not a list.
program
trace r
if .nil then nop
say 'after'
----
rc> 222
err>      2 *-* if .nil 
err>        >>>   "The NIL object"
err>      2 *-* if .nil 
err> Error 34 running /nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex line 2:  Logical value not 0 or 1.
err> Error 34.1:  Value of expression following IF keyword must be exactly "0" or "1"; found "The NIL object".

# a declining condition under trace that does not refuse: a comma list prints
# a `>>>` per element it evaluates, from `eval_logical_list`, and one more for
# the list's own result, from the tail every condition enters. Measured, this
# row catches a second validating op emitted behind the fallback -- the ir arm
# prints a fourth `>>>` the oracle does not -- where the row above raises
# before such an op could run at all.
program
trace r
if 1, 1 then say 'both'
say 'after'
----
rc> 0
out> both
out> after
err>      2 *-* if 1, 1 
err>        >>>   "1"
err>        >>>   "1"
err>        >>>   "1"
err>      2 *-*   then
err>      2 *-*     say 'both'
err>        >>>       "both"
err>      3 *-* say 'after'
err>        >>>   "after"

